1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,959
Every time you open a website, a request travels across the internet and back.

2
00:00:05,509 --> 00:00:09,135
It takes less than a second, but it happens in several clear steps.

3
00:00:09,685 --> 00:00:14,229
Today you trace one request, step by step, and watch the steps happen live.

4
00:00:14,779 --> 00:00:18,577
Then, when a page will not load, you will know exactly where to look.

5
00:00:20,627 --> 00:00:22,078
Here is the whole journey.

6
00:00:22,628 --> 00:00:26,020
One: you type a name, like shop dot example dot com.

7
00:00:26,570 --> 00:00:29,663
Two: DNS turns that name into an IP address.

8
00:00:30,213 --> 00:00:34,650
Three: your browser opens a TCP connection to that address, on a port.

9
00:00:35,200 --> 00:00:37,675
Four: it sends an HTTP request.

10
00:00:38,225 --> 00:00:42,492
Five: the server builds a response, often by asking an application.

11
00:00:43,042 --> 00:00:46,306
Six: the response travels back, and the page appears.

12
00:00:46,856 --> 00:00:49,693
Each step can fail, and each failure looks different.

13
00:00:51,743 --> 00:00:53,642
Step two: from a name to a number.

14
00:00:54,192 --> 00:00:57,221
Computers connect by IP address, not by name.

15
00:00:57,771 --> 00:01:01,568
DNS is the phone book. It turns a name into an address.

16
00:01:02,118 --> 00:01:05,126
If DNS fails, you see: could not resolve host.

17
00:01:05,676 --> 00:01:07,938
The first check: can the name be resolved?

18
00:01:09,988 --> 00:01:12,996
Let us try a name that this computer has never heard of.

19
00:01:13,546 --> 00:01:16,767
Type: curl web:5000/health

20
00:01:16,897 --> 00:01:21,932
Could not resolve host. The name has no address, so the request never even starts.

21
00:01:22,482 --> 00:01:28,306
Now we hand curl the address ourselves.

22
00:01:28,306 --> 00:01:36,519
Type: curl --resolve web:5000:127.0.0.1 web:5000/health

23
00:01:36,649 --> 00:01:39,743
That option says: when you look for web, use this address.

24
00:01:40,293 --> 00:01:45,263
A reply, ok! Once the name had an address, the request reached our application.

25
00:01:45,813 --> 00:01:51,232
Your computer already knows one name by itself. Type: getent hosts localhost

26
00:01:51,362 --> 00:01:53,963
Localhost always means this computer.

27
00:01:53,963 --> 00:01:58,039
Its address is one twenty-seven dot zero dot zero dot one.

28
00:02:00,089 --> 00:02:02,436
Step four: the HTTP request.

29
00:02:02,986 --> 00:02:05,631
A request has a method, a path, and headers.

30
00:02:06,181 --> 00:02:11,386
The method says what to do. GET reads something. POST sends data.

31
00:02:11,936 --> 00:02:13,600
The path says which thing you want.

32
00:02:14,150 --> 00:02:16,838
Headers add details, such as which host you mean.

33
00:02:17,388 --> 00:02:21,122
The response comes back with a status code, headers, and a body.

34
00:02:23,172 --> 00:02:28,633
Let us catch a real request. We ask for the health page, and show only the lines we send.

35
00:02:29,183 --> 00:02:38,143
Type: curl -sv localhost:5000/health 2>&1 | grep '^>'

36
00:02:38,273 --> 00:02:44,353
The first line is the request: method GET, path slash health, protocol HTTP.

37
00:02:44,903 --> 00:02:47,378
Next, the host header: which server we mean.

38
00:02:47,928 --> 00:02:53,624
Now the lines that came back. Type: curl -sv localhost:5000/health 2>&1 | grep '^<'

39
00:02:53,754 --> 00:02:56,655
The first line is the status: two hundred, O K.

40
00:02:57,205 --> 00:03:00,171
The content type header says what kind of data follows.

41
00:03:00,721 --> 00:03:06,865
And the body itself. Type: curl -s localhost:5000/health

42
00:03:06,995 --> 00:03:10,177
A small piece of data that says the status is ok.

43
00:03:10,177 --> 00:03:12,904
That is one complete request and response.

44
00:03:14,954 --> 00:03:16,746
Step five: the status code.

45
00:03:17,296 --> 00:03:20,325
Read the first digit. It tells you who has the problem.

46
00:03:20,875 --> 00:03:22,710
Two hundred range: success.

47
00:03:23,260 --> 00:03:25,372
Three hundred range: go somewhere else.

48
00:03:25,922 --> 00:03:30,594
Four hundred range: the request is wrong, such as a page that does not exist.

49
00:03:31,144 --> 00:03:33,491
Five hundred range: the server failed.

50
00:03:35,541 --> 00:03:39,827
Let us ask our application three questions.

51
00:03:39,827 --> 00:03:41,921
First: is it healthy?

52
00:03:41,921 --> 00:03:46,506
Type: curl -si localhost:5000/health | head -1

53
00:03:46,636 --> 00:03:47,959
Two hundred, O K.

54
00:03:48,509 --> 00:03:52,541
Now a page that does not exist. Type: curl -si localhost:5000/nope | head -1

55
00:03:52,671 --> 00:03:54,847
Four oh four. The request was wrong.

56
00:03:55,397 --> 00:03:58,293
Now a request that makes the application itself fail.

57
00:03:58,293 --> 00:04:00,752
Type: curl -si localhost:5000/crash | head -1

58
00:04:00,882 --> 00:04:05,767
Five hundred. The server failed. Different first digit, different owner of the problem.

59
00:04:07,817 --> 00:04:09,182
Who builds the response?

60
00:04:09,732 --> 00:04:14,596
A web server, like nginx, hands out files and passes other requests along.

61
00:04:15,146 --> 00:04:19,626
An application server runs your code. It looks up data and builds the page.

62
00:04:20,176 --> 00:04:25,126
Often they sit in a chain: browser, web server, application server, database.

63
00:04:25,676 --> 00:04:30,113
A five oh two or five oh three often means the chain is broken in the middle.

64
00:04:32,163 --> 00:04:35,180
We can measure each step of the journey.

65
00:04:35,180 --> 00:04:38,121
A small file tells curl what to report.

66
00:04:38,121 --> 00:04:39,630
Type: cat timing.txt

67
00:04:39,760 --> 00:04:44,794
Name lookup, connect, first byte, and total. The backslash n just means a new line.

68
00:04:45,344 --> 00:04:49,460
Now run the request with that report.

69
00:04:49,460 --> 00:04:56,246
Type: curl -so /dev/null -w @timing.txt localhost:5000/health

70
00:04:56,376 --> 00:05:00,685
Every step took a tiny fraction of a second. This is a healthy request.

71
00:05:01,235 --> 00:05:04,883
Now a page that is slow. Type: curl -so /dev/null -w @timing.txt localhost:5000/slow

72
00:05:06,513 --> 00:05:10,054
Look at first byte. Two seconds! But connect was still tiny.

73
00:05:10,604 --> 00:05:12,049
So the network was fine.

74
00:05:12,049 --> 00:05:14,215
The delay is inside the application.

75
00:05:14,215 --> 00:05:16,322
The timing tells you where to look.

76
00:05:18,372 --> 00:05:20,825
Use the trace as a checklist, from the start.

77
00:05:21,375 --> 00:05:24,085
Name not found? That is a DNS problem.

78
00:05:24,635 --> 00:05:27,707
Connection refused? Nothing is listening on that port.

79
00:05:28,257 --> 00:05:30,817
A four hundred range code? Fix the request.

80
00:05:31,367 --> 00:05:35,100
A five hundred range code? Look at the application and its logs.

81
00:05:35,650 --> 00:05:39,490
Slow? Compare connect with first byte, to find which side is slow.

82
00:05:41,540 --> 00:05:43,119
Match the symptom to the step.

83
00:05:43,669 --> 00:05:45,845
Could not resolve host: DNS.

84
00:05:46,395 --> 00:05:49,168
Connection refused: no service on that port.

85
00:05:49,718 --> 00:05:51,361
Four oh four: wrong path.

86
00:05:51,911 --> 00:05:54,215
Five hundred: the application failed.

87
00:05:54,765 --> 00:05:59,351
Five oh two or five oh three: the web server cannot reach the application.

88
00:06:01,401 --> 00:06:03,257
Watch out for four common myths.

89
00:06:03,807 --> 00:06:08,394
Myth one: four oh four means the site is down. It means the server answered.

90
00:06:08,944 --> 00:06:13,296
Myth two: five hundred errors are the browser's fault. They come from the server.

91
00:06:13,846 --> 00:06:19,158
Myth three: slow always means a slow network. The timing may show a slow application.

92
00:06:19,708 --> 00:06:24,081
Myth four: one error code means one cause. Always confirm in the logs.

93
00:06:24,631 --> 00:06:26,316
Trace first, then conclude.

94
00:06:28,366 --> 00:06:30,691
Here is how you check your own understanding.

95
00:06:31,241 --> 00:06:33,609
List the six steps of a request, in order.

96
00:06:34,159 --> 00:06:37,509
Explain what DNS does, and show a resolve failure.

97
00:06:38,059 --> 00:06:41,621
Read a request line and a response line with curl dash v.

98
00:06:42,171 --> 00:06:45,478
Say who is at fault from the first digit of a status code.

99
00:06:46,028 --> 00:06:48,503
Use the timing report to find a slow step.

100
00:06:49,053 --> 00:06:51,357
Name the first check for each kind of failure.

101
00:06:51,907 --> 00:06:53,528
To pass, you need all six.

102
00:06:55,578 --> 00:06:57,861
Your turn, in the virtual machine you built.

103
00:06:58,411 --> 00:07:03,168
One: start the Python web server from last time, and fetch it with curl dash v.

104
00:07:03,718 --> 00:07:07,302
Two: find the request line and the status line in the output.

105
00:07:07,852 --> 00:07:11,799
Three: ask for a page that does not exist, and read the four oh four.

106
00:07:12,349 --> 00:07:16,018
Four: try curl on a made-up name, and read the DNS error.

107
00:07:16,568 --> 00:07:19,853
Five: use the timing report, and write down each number.

108
00:07:20,403 --> 00:07:23,710
Mark yourself on the rubric. Pause the video, and do it now.

109
00:07:39,260 --> 00:07:40,135
Quick check.

110
00:07:40,685 --> 00:07:42,733
Name the six steps of a web request.

111
00:07:43,283 --> 00:07:44,691
What does DNS do?

112
00:07:45,241 --> 00:07:47,950
What does the first digit of a status code tell you?

113
00:07:48,500 --> 00:07:51,935
How can timing show whether the network or the application is slow?

114
00:07:52,485 --> 00:07:56,943
